<!-- cell: title-page id="9f966cb2-86b8-4696-a51a-9c071786f597" title="The Same Set" -->

<!-- cell: markdown id="af49aed8-6dc5-4e51-b0d5-4d100e1fb3b5" -->

Every Thursday at four, Lia climbed the stairs of the Café Zsolnay to the back room where old men had played chess for a hundred years under a ceiling stained brown by their cigarettes.

Benedek was always there first, setting up the pieces with a teacher's precision. He was eighty-one, had taught mathematics for forty years, and had lost to almost no one in that room for longer than that. Lia was a philosophy student with no money and a stubborn Sicilian Defence. She came to understand the game, not to win it, which was as well, because he beat her every week with an air of quiet apology.

In her second winter she noticed the pieces. The white king was pale boxwood, the queen almost amber, one knight had a chipped ear, and one rook was plainly plastic. "Your set doesn't match," she said.

"It used to," said Benedek. "In 1961 it was perfect. Then a pawn fell into a coffee, a bishop went home in a child's pocket, and the king went into the stove one cold February. I have replaced every piece. Not one of the first set is left."

"Then is it the same set?" He smiled the way he smiled when she walked into a trap. "You are the philosopher. You tell me."

She thought while he took her bishop. "If it's the pieces, then no. But nobody in this room would call it a different set. They'd call it Benedek's set. So maybe a set is the games played on it, not the wood."

"Maybe," said Benedek. Then, carefully, as if making a move he could not take back, he said: "The doctor says I am forgetting things. Names first. Then other things. He showed me pictures of my brain. Pieces going, one by one."

Around them cups clinked and old men muttered over their clocks, as they had every afternoon since before either of them was born. "I wonder," Benedek said, "whether at the end there will be anyone left who is the same man, or only someone sitting in his chair."

She had no answer, and he did not seem to expect one. They finished the game, and he won, as always.

On the last Thursday of March he was not there. On the table lay the chess set in its old wooden box, with a card in his precise hand: For Lia. It is still the same set. You remember the games.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="98ef5caa-7b2b-4c0e-a806-0d32a05655f0" title="Benedek" words="34-69, 145-192, 199-217, 260-300, 324-348, 349-369, 370-379" -->

An eighty-one-year-old former mathematics teacher and chess player, courteous and proud, who is losing his memory. He fears ceasing to be himself before he stops living, and his last move is to hand on what matters.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="0bbe6d35-b6c9-4176-8462-e64bc7003654" title="Lia" words="0-33, 70-108, 109-144, 193-198, 218-259, 349-369, 380-410" -->

A philosophy student, poor and stubborn, who plays Benedek every Thursday and always loses. She wants to understand, not to win, and becomes the keeper of his games.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="059bfed5-91cf-40fb-93f2-6443a0b0393e" title="The chess room of the Café Zsolnay" words="0-33, 34-69, 218-259, 301-323" -->

The smoke-stained back room of a Budapest café where old men have played chess for a century: a place that stays the same while its players come and go.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="68e8d9dd-3810-4f52-9b8b-f817e94f5033" title="Identity" words="109-144, 145-192, 193-217, 218-259, 324-348, 380-410" -->

Whether a thing whose every part has been replaced is still the same thing: the chess set, and Benedek himself.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="a9736356-fc6e-4fa9-ba03-1f593ad4d26d" title="Memory" words="260-300, 324-348, 370-410" -->

Benedek's illness and what survives it: he is forgetting, names first and then more, though the story never names the disease, and the games live on in Lia.

<!-- cell: plot id="5f0b6122-3588-4eae-9b5d-3a2df647d20a" title="The same set" words="0-33, 109-144, 145-192, 193-198, 218-259, 260-300, 324-348, 370-410" -->

Lia notices the mismatched pieces; Benedek tells her every piece has been replaced and asks whether it is the same set; she answers that a set is its games; he reveals he is losing his memory; on the last Thursday he leaves her the set.
